If the perimeter of one of the triangles is 21 inches, the perp of the hexagon is $21 + 3(21) = 84$, the second coefficient is $3$. If the perimeter of one of the triangles is $31$ inches, the perimeter of the hexagon is $31 + 3(31) =124$. Is there a general formula to get this without trial and error?
Taking a step back, let each equilateral triangle have a side length of $a$, so the perimeter of one of the equilateral triangles is $3a$. If the perimeter of the hexagon is three times greater, we have $9a=3(3a)$. So, if the perimeter of the equilateral triangle is 21 inches, the perimeter of the hexagon is $21+21(3)=72$ inches, which is $\boxed{\frac{d}{6}}$ inches. If the perimeter of the equilateral triangle is $31$ inches, the perimeter of the hexagon is $31+31(3)=124$ inches, which is $\boxed{\frac{d}{6}}$ inches.

Why is multiplication easier than addition, especially in the context of numbers represented in different numeral systems? We can view multiplication by a positive integer $d$ as successive addition. This is important because it lets us very easily convert to other numeral systems. The basic idea of the conversion algorithm is that to convert to base $d$, we repeatedly divide by $d$ and record the remainders.
For example, to convert to base $5$, we can convert $123$. First, $123 \div 5 = 24$ with a remainder of $3$. Writing the remainder down and recording the divisor (24) for future division, we have
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
24 & \div & 5 & \text{ rem } & 4\\
\big\downarrow & & \big\downarrow & & \big\downarrow\\
\end{array}
\]so the rightmost digit of the base $5$ representation of $123$ is $3$ (instead of $4$).

Then, since $4 \div 5 = 0$ with a remainder of $4$,(x
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
4 & \div& 5 &\text{ rem }& 4
\\ \big\downarrow& &\big\downarrow& &\big\downarrow\\
\end{array}
\]so our base $5$ number is $b_2b_1b_0 = \boxed{433}_5$.) The actual remainder recorded is the digit of the remainders array which are written down as numbers (alternatively, we may read off the recursive definition of division: $r=dq+n$; then $na<bd^2+r<bd^2+d^2=(b+1)d^2$ so $n\le2\cdot9$). Each of these steps is easy if we're representing digits using an array.

